schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: "Gas Fee (EIP-1559 Base Fee + Tip Model)"
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    The cost of processing a transaction on a blockchain, expressed as Gas
    Used × (Base Fee + Tip). Since Ethereum's EIP-1559 upgrade (2021), the
    base fee auto-adjusts with network demand and is burned; the tip
    (priority fee) is an optional extra to jump the queue.
  source_traditions:
    - Ethereum protocol economics (EIP-1559, 2021)
    - Pre-EIP-1559 first-price auction gas markets (the older model this replaced)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Explained here as foundational vocabulary (gas, gas price/gwei, gas fee,
    gas limit) every newcomer needs before transacting, with Layer 2 networks
    positioned as the practical fee-reduction path.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - >-
      Bitcoin transaction fees (a simpler, non-EIP-1559 fee-market model not
      discussed in this article)
    - Layer 2 gas fees (same base concept, much lower absolute cost, per the article)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      Base fee (burned, algorithmically set) vs. priority fee/tip (paid to
      the validator, user-set) — conflating the two obscures why "network
      busy" and "I set a high tip" are different levers on cost.
  tensions: []
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Describing gas simply as "gasoline for your car" is a useful first
    analogy but risks obscuring that gas price is a live auction/algorithmic
    market, not a fixed unit price the way fuel is.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/gas-fees.md
    transformation: synthesized
    authorship: ai-assisted
